# Kimi-K3 Validation Gates

Use staged validation for any Kimi-K3 kernel or serving optimization:

1. Start with focused dispatch/fallback tests and numerical microbenchmarks on
   the target architecture. Fail immediately on wrong dispatch, NaN/Inf, or a
   numerical threshold violation.
2. Benchmark graph replay when the production path uses CUDA/HIP graphs.
   Include conversion/cast work that production performs.
3. Attribute changes within one backend before the final backend A/B.
4. For production comparisons, use the current production profile as baseline,
   not merely the old implementation of the candidate backend.
5. Keep model, TP, KV dtype, cache state, prompts, seeds, chunk size, request
   limits, warmups, and all unrelated optimization flags identical.
6. Run fixed 8K/1K and 68K/350 endpoint workloads over the required
   concurrencies. Save throughput, TTFT, TPOT, successes, OOMs, and retractions.
7. Run paired GSM8K and long-context output/logprob checks after performance
   measurement. State clearly when endpoint top-k probabilities are used as a
   proxy rather than full logits.
8. Do not promote an optimization that misses any required performance or
   correctness gate. Keep it default-off and document the rejection.
9. Save raw evidence under `/workspace/kimi-k3-runs/` and the decision under
   `/workspace/claude-skills/kimi-k3/aiter-optimization-tracker/`.
